
ViewModelScope — Theory

1) viewModelScope is a CoroutineScope tied to ViewModel lifecycle.

2) It automatically cancels all coroutines when:
   → ViewModel.onCleared() is called.

3) ViewModel survives:
   → Configuration changes (rotation).

4) ViewModel is destroyed when:
   → The owning Activity or Fragment is FINISHED.

5) viewModelScope is best suited for:
   → Business logic
   → Network calls
   → Database operations

6) viewModelScope.launch { } uses:
   → Dispatchers.Main by default

7) Prevents memory leaks by following structured concurrency.




viewModelScope.launch { }

1) launch starts a new coroutine.
2) Coroutine runs as long as ViewModel is alive.
3) Automatically cancelled when ViewModel is cleared.
4) No need to manually cancel the coroutine.
5) Encouraged by Android Architecture Components.

